From 0c5b90ff0a2c746da2a23cf4c8fc565e8ed37e47 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Viacheslav Bocharov <v@baodeep.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 15:14:21 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(telegram): add a dice action to the message tool

Telegram sendDice plays an animated roll and picks the outcome
server-side, so a roll a bot posts is verifiable by everyone who watches
the animation. Posting the emoji as text only produces a static picture,
and the Bot API method was unreachable from OpenClaw.

Add dice to the core action vocabulary with target mode "to" and no
conversation read, plus a scoped schema group carrying one optional emoji
param, so a dice-only tool surface advertises the six faces Telegram
supports instead of none. The Telegram runtime wraps sendDice and returns
messageId, chatId, emoji, and the rolled value, so the caller learns the
outcome from the same tool result instead of re-reading the chat. The
action rides the existing channels.telegram.actions.sendMessage gate
instead of carrying one of its own: a roll is an ordinary outbound message
to a chat the bot may already post in.

Two shared classifications every other recipient-visible send belongs to
are completed here. Cross-context egress: enforceCrossContextPolicy returns
before comparing targets for an action missing from CONTEXT_GUARDED_ACTIONS,
so a bound run could roll into another configured chat with cross-context
delivery disabled. Terminal delivery: a roll delivered to the run's own
conversation was not recognised as the run's visible reply, so the run sent
a redundant fallback reply after the roll had landed; mirrorable payload
eligibility is now separate from target-addressed delivery classification,
which also keeps poll params out of the transcript. The handler forwards
the silent option its schema already advertised.

Upstream: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/138202
